## 5. Magnet Links — Torrents Without .torrent Files

### What a Magnet Link Is

A magnet link is a URI scheme that encodes enough information to start a torrent download without having a .torrent file. The browser or OS passes it to your client, which then fetches the metadata from the swarm itself.

### Anatomy of a Magnet Link

```
magnet:?xt=urn:btih:2A47DA3A8B2D4F5A9E12C8B3F47A8D3F2A1E9C4B
       &dn=Ubuntu+22.04+Desktop
       &tr=udp%3A%2F%2Ftracker.opentrackr.org%3A1337%2Fannounce
       &xl=1234567890
```

**`xt`** (exact topic) — the info hash, encoded as `urn:btih:` followed by either a 40-character hex string or a 32-character Base32 string. This is the only required parameter.

**`dn`** (display name) — URL-encoded display name. Optional hint for the UI.

**`tr`** (tracker) — URL-encoded tracker URL. Can appear multiple times for multiple trackers.

**`xl`** (exact length) — file size in bytes. Optional.

**`as`** (acceptable source) — a direct download URL for the file. Rarely used.

**`ws`** (web seed) — a URL from which file data can be fetched via HTTP (BitTorrent web seeding, BEP 19).

### How Your Client Resolves a Magnet Link

1. Extract the info hash from `xt`.
2. If `tr` is present, query those trackers immediately to get an initial peer list.
3. Query the DHT for the info hash to find peers.
4. Connect to any peer you find. Use the extension protocol (BEP 9) to request the `ut_metadata` extension.
5. Request the metadata (the bencoded `info` dictionary) in 16 KB blocks from peers.
6. Once you have all metadata blocks, verify by computing SHA-1 and comparing to the info hash.
7. Now you have the full torrent metadata and can proceed normally.

This is called "metadata exchange" and is specified in BEP 9.

