## 13. uTP — The Transport Layer

### Why uTP Exists

Standard BitTorrent uses TCP. TCP has no mechanism for yielding to other traffic. When you're torrenting aggressively, your TCP connections compete equally with video calls, web browsing, and games — often winning, because BitTorrent opens many connections. This is the cause of the "BitTorrent kills my internet" phenomenon.

uTP (Micro Transport Protocol, BEP 29) is a UDP-based transport protocol designed specifically for BitTorrent. It implements its own congestion control algorithm (LEDBAT) that actively detects network congestion and backs off, allowing other traffic to proceed unimpeded.

### LEDBAT: Congestion Control

LEDBAT (Low Extra Delay Background Transport) works by measuring the *delay increase* it causes, not just packet loss.

The idea: if your packets are arriving with increasing latency, you are filling a buffer somewhere. Back off before the buffer overflows.

```
target_delay = 25 ms  (your goal: never add more than 25ms of queuing delay)
current_delay = measured one-way delay from receiver
delay_queue = measure delay, subtract base delay (best case, unloaded path)

if delay_queue > target_delay:
    # We're adding too much delay — shrink congestion window
    cwnd -= alpha * (delay_queue - target_delay)
else:
    # We have headroom — grow window slightly
    cwnd += max_cwnd_increase_per_rtt * packet_size / cwnd
```

The result: uTP fills spare bandwidth greedily but immediately yields whenever latency-sensitive traffic (TCP, VoIP, gaming) needs the link.

### uTP Packet Format

uTP is layered over UDP. Each uTP packet has a 20-byte header:

```
Byte 0:  type (3 bits) + version (4 bits)
         Types: ST_DATA=0, ST_FIN=1, ST_STATE=2, ST_RESET=3, ST_SYN=4
Byte 1:  extension (0 = no extension)
Byte 2-3: connection_id (uint16)
Byte 4-7: timestamp_microseconds (uint32): sender's time
Byte 8-11: timestamp_difference_microseconds (uint32): delay measurement
Byte 12-15: wnd_size: receiver's available buffer
Byte 16-17: seq_nr: sequence number
Byte 18-19: ack_nr: acknowledgment number
```

uTP implements: connection setup (SYN/SYN-ACK), selective ACKs (SACK), retransmission, flow control (window-based), and connection teardown (FIN). It is essentially a reimplementation of TCP's transport guarantees in UDP with better congestion control.

**For your client:** You can use TCP exclusively and still interoperate with all peers. uTP is an optimization. Many clients offer both and prefer uTP when the remote peer supports it.

