## 15. Extension Protocol (BEP 10)

### Overview

The Extension Protocol (BEP 10) defines a generic mechanism for adding new message types to the peer wire protocol without breaking backward compatibility.

During the handshake, you signal support for BEP 10 by setting bit 20 (from the right) in the reserved bytes: `reserved[5] |= 0x10`.

### The Handshake Message (Message ID 20)

After the standard handshake, both peers that support BEP 10 send an extension handshake message:

```
Message ID: 20
Extension message ID: 0  (0 = handshake)
Payload: bencoded dictionary
```

The handshake dictionary advertises which extensions you support and assigns local IDs:

```python
{
    "m": {
        "ut_pex": 1,       # Peer exchange (BEP 11)
        "ut_metadata": 2,  # Metadata exchange (BEP 9, for magnet links)
        "ut_holepunch": 3  # Hole punching (BEP 55)
    },
    "v": "MyClient 1.0",   # client version string
    "yourip": <4 bytes>,   # peer's IP as seen by you
    "reqq": 255,           # how many outstanding requests you'll accept
    "metadata_size": 12345 # size of info dict (if you have it)
}
```

The `"m"` dictionary maps extension names to local message IDs. These IDs are local to this connection — the same extension might be ID 1 on one connection and ID 3 on another. You always check the handshake to know which ID to use.

### ut_metadata (BEP 9)

Used to fetch the `info` dictionary from peers, enabling magnet link resolution. Messages:

```python
# Request a 16KB chunk of the metadata
{"msg_type": 0, "piece": 0}

# Receive a chunk
{"msg_type": 1, "piece": 0, "total_size": 12345}
# followed by raw bytes of this chunk
```

There are `ceil(metadata_size / 16384)` pieces. Request them all, concatenate, and verify the SHA-1 against the info hash.

